The View system that powers Android apps has been in use since the very beginning of Android. For the most part, it has been very stable. But as apps grow in complexity, the View system can be riddled with problems when you don’t know how to use it correctly.
As you know, Android is a living, breathing ecosystem. Functionality that was once forbidden has now become common practice (also known as “best practices”). This article explores the pitfalls associated with the View system in Android and how to avoid them with Compose.
Pitfall: View recreation on recomposition
The very first codelab I took on Compose was View Composition . It showed how to use Compose to react to changes in a layout in a view hierarchy using ViewModifier . This pattern was an easy way to let Compose react to changes in a view hierarchy, without waiting for Java code to call invalidate() .
However, it’s easy to create a scenario where a view is created and recreated every single recomposition. This causes the following problems:
Essential work is done on the UI thread
Work is done in an unstable environment
There’s no opportunity to optimize work
The best way to determine if a given ViewModifier is being recreated every single recomposition is to look at the View hierarchy. If a view is being created and destroyed in every single recomposition, it’s time to look for ways to make the ViewModifier stable.
How can I make my ViewModifier stable?
Compose uses lambdas, so you can make your ViewModifier stable by making the inner lambda passed to it stable.
For example, if you have a lambda that’s instant, it’s stable:
val viewModifier = remember { Modifier.background(Color.Red) }
viewModifier is stable because what it does and how it works doesn’t change over time. If you need to pass a Color value as a parameter to the Modifier , then you need to make sure this parameter is stable. For example, if you use a value from a ViewModel or a variable that can change over time, this will make your ViewModifier unstable.
Compose provides StableRefs , which can be used to store stable data, and StableLayoutModifier , which can be used to make your ViewModifier stable. For more information, see Stable layout references .
How do I know if my ViewModifier is stable?
The best way to know if your ViewModifier is stable is to add log statements to it and confirm that the View hierarchy is not growing. I prefer to use the .sizeIn() modifier to get a count of the number of views being created in a layout. For example:
