ode() return different Int value</li></ul> <p> These values don't mean anything beyond View.MeasureSpec. Instead, they are the result of some upstream calculation. <p> So, what do we do with View.MeasureSpec.getSize()? We pass it to View.measure(int, int) to assign an actual size to a View. <p> The ViewMeasureSpecs are a result of View.MeasureSpec.getSize() and are not real dimensions. <p> The value of View.MeasureSpec.getSize() is not a real dimension, it's a View.MeasureSpec. <p> Let's take a closer look at how View.MeasureSpec.getSize() can return different Int values when working with View.matchParent() and View.wrapContent(). <p> fitIn(match_parent) vs fitIn(wrap_content) fitIn(match_parent) vs fitIn(wrap_content) <p><table> <thead><th style="border: 1px solid grey;" width="200px">fitIn()</th><th style="border: 1px solid grey;" width="150px">View.MeasureSpec</th><th style="border: 1px solid grey;" width="250px">View.measure(int, int)</th></thead><tbody><tr><th style="border: 1px solid grey;" width="200px">WrapContent()</th><td style="border: 1px solid grey;" width="150px">View.MeasureSpec.getSize()=xxx<br>View.MeasureSpec.getMode()=View.MeasureSpec.AT_MOST<td style="border: 1px solid grey;" width="250px">View.measure(int, int)<br>real size = xxx<br>real size = AT_MOST<br>View.setMeasuredDimension(0, 0)<br>call to invalidate() preferred.</th></td>
