A view that lets the user enter and edit text. Unlike UITextField , UITextView is a subclass of UIView and therefore can be used not only in interface builder but also in code. UITextView is much more flexible than UITextField as it is a much more complex control to begin with. For example, UITextView lets you select multiple lines of text and even magnify and move this text around. UITextField can't do any of that. Also, UITextViews can have line wrapping, while UITextFields always assume they are one line controls.
A Text View is built upon a Text Storage object that represents the actual text data. This text storage can be a single or a composition of text storage objects. For example, by default it is composed of a String Storage object that contains the actual text, an NSTextAttachment cell storage that contains the attachment objects that are visible in the text, and an NSFontManager font storage that contains the font information. This text storage is then represented by an NSTextContainer object, which in turn is used by an NSLayoutManager to break up the text into visible lines, and then these lines are drawn to the screen.
The text view itself is supported by an NSTextContainerSizeCache that allows the text view to adapt its size to the content, and an NSTextContainerLineFittingStrategy that allows the text view to provide a strategy to support the text container's size cache.
Select a text view and open the Size inspector. In the Layout section, you can set the percentage that the text view should occupy in its parent view. This value is relative to all other subviews of the same parent view.
You can also set these values by using the setPreferredMaxLayoutWidth: method of the UITextView class.
If the text view is not laying out its content with respect to its preferred maximum width (as defined by the text view's preferredMaxLayoutWidth property), the text view's height will not be calculated correctly when used inside a horizontal stack view.
These methods are useful when using the text view to display a long passage of text.
